1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,605
Placement season, reimagined.

2
00:00:02,967 --> 00:00:07,388
Meet AAFT Placements,
a Space on TwinCafe.

3
00:00:08,310 --> 00:00:11,288
A Space gives a community its own Twin,

4
00:00:11,328 --> 00:00:14,655
private to its members,
trained on its documents,

5
00:00:14,695 --> 00:00:16,342
with agents built in.

6
00:00:17,510 --> 00:00:20,898
One Space for the whole batch
and the placement cell.

7
00:00:21,510 --> 00:00:25,049
A student asks which companies
are coming in December.

8
00:00:25,260 --> 00:00:29,125
The Space answers from the
placement cell's own drive calendar.

9
00:00:29,910 --> 00:00:34,494
Another asks what Meridian Analytics
will ask, and how to prepare.

10
00:00:34,890 --> 00:00:39,660
The answer draws on seniors' debriefs
from past Meridian interviews.

11
00:00:41,010 --> 00:00:44,724
Every recruiter comes with
its own mock interview.

12
00:00:44,934 --> 00:00:47,998
Students pick a company
and start practising.

13
00:00:48,810 --> 00:00:53,542
Students upload their CV, and get asked
about their experience

14
00:00:53,582 --> 00:00:55,752
for the role they're applying to.

15
00:00:56,550 --> 00:01:00,751
You cut cost per install
from ₹38 to ₹24.

16
00:01:01,240 --> 00:01:03,143
Were those listeners worth keeping?

17
00:01:03,693 --> 00:01:07,769
Not at first. So I started judging
creatives on day-seven retention.

18
00:01:09,210 --> 00:01:11,863
They can practise all types of interviews,

19
00:01:11,903 --> 00:01:16,870
like case interviews, technical rounds,
HR discussions and more.

20
00:01:18,010 --> 00:01:22,359
Week one. Option A costs ₹20 an install,

21
00:01:22,399 --> 00:01:25,021
and 4% reach a paid episode.

22
00:01:25,301 --> 00:01:29,490
Option B costs ₹32, and 14% do.

23
00:01:29,980 --> 00:01:31,232
Which do you scale?

24
00:01:31,750 --> 00:01:36,973
B. ₹229 per paying listener,
against ₹500 for A.

25
00:01:38,110 --> 00:01:39,916
From marketing to cinema,

26
00:01:39,956 --> 00:01:43,065
every school practises
for its own roles.

27
00:01:43,910 --> 00:01:45,925
Your lead actor is 90 minutes late,

28
00:01:45,965 --> 00:01:48,030
and you lose the light at 5:30.

29
00:01:48,542 --> 00:01:49,806
What do you do?

30
00:01:50,006 --> 00:01:53,774
First, I tell the first AD to reshuffle
to the scenes we can shoot without him,

31
00:01:53,814 --> 00:01:55,600
so we don't lose the light.

32
00:01:56,710 --> 00:01:59,062
Every attempt ends with a scorecard,

33
00:01:59,102 --> 00:02:02,212
a recording and what to practise next.

34
00:02:02,608 --> 00:02:05,869
Students practise again
as often as they like.

35
00:02:07,210 --> 00:02:11,179
Every question answered.
Every interview practised.

36
00:02:11,576 --> 00:02:14,314
Placement-ready, around the clock.

